    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1. Assemble and clean the observations\n",
    "\n",
    "This step gathers the field observations that support later steps. The inventory supplies stem-level ecological labels and measurements; plot polygons define where those measurements were collected; the trait table supports biomass estimation; and the NEON Airborne Observation Platform (AOP) supplies lidar and hyperspectral coverage for the same site and flight period.\n",
    "\n",
    "The goal is not merely to download files. It is to establish a defensible link between a field observation and the remote-sensing pixels that will eventually be classified."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6ebcfd22-6628-4b22-a436-f78bf33db5af",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<img src=\"docs/1_workflow.png\" width=\"90%\">\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1a. Download and clean forest inventory data\n",
    "\n",
    "**Functions:** `download_veg_structure_data`, `download_polygons`, `download_trait_table`, `prep_veg_structure`, `prep_polygons`\n",
    "\n",
    "The first code block downloads the field and airborne inputs. Stem-level inventory records (DP1.10098.001) include species or taxonomic identity, diameter at breast height (DBH), height, and location. Plot polygons and sampling-effort information provide the footprint and sampling context of those measurements. The trait table provides attributes needed by the allometric biomass equations used later."
   ]
  },
