    "### 2a. Prepare lidar for plot-level analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "**Functions:** `normalize_laz`, `clip_lidar_by_plots`\n",
    "\n",
    "lidar is a three-dimensional point cloud. Each return records where the airborne laser interacted with the ground, understory, branches, or canopy. Raw elevation mixes terrain height and vegetation height, so the workflow first subtracts a digital terrain model. After normalization, the vertical coordinate can be interpreted as height above ground.\n",
    "\n",
    "The normalized point cloud is then clipped to the partitioned plot boundaries. This is the key spatial join between airborne structure and field observations: the lidar points used to characterize a plot are drawn from the same area in which the inventory was collected.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Expected product:** normalized, plot-clipped lidar point clouds suitable for deriving vertical canopy metrics.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Where this product is generated:** the first code call below invokes `normalize_laz`, defined in `initialize/lidar.py`. It passes the downloaded AOP lidar directory (`laz_path`) and writes terrain-normalized `.laz` tiles to:\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "${TUTORIAL_HOME}/data/intermediate/TEAK/2021/normalized_lidar_tiles/\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The normalization itself is performed by the R helper `normalize_lidR` in `initialize/leaf_area_density_helper.R`. It uses `lidR::normalize_height()` with a triangulated irregular network (`tin()`) built from ground returns. This removes terrain elevation from each point's Z coordinate. The input files remain unchanged in:\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "${TUTORIAL_HOME}/data/raw/aop/TEAK/2021-07/laz/\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The second code call invokes `clip_lidar_by_plots`, passing the normalized directory, the AOP raster directory, and the partitioned plot polygons generated in Step 1. The R helper `clip_lidar_to_polygon_lidR` clips the normalized catalog to the plot polygons and writes plot-level files, named from the plot and subplot identifiers, to:\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "${TUTORIAL_HOME}/data/intermediate/TEAK/2021/clipped_to_plots/\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The `clipped_to_plots` directory holds the product that will be used by the next Leaf Area Density (LAD) step. The function also uses an intermediate `clipped_lidar_tiles/` directory while merging and processing tile-level files; for the workshop's final plot-level output, look in `clipped_to_plots/` for the `.laz` files."
   ]
  },
