    "### 2b. Derive canopy structure from leaf area density\n",
    "\n",
    "**Function:** `prep_lad`\n",
    "\n",
    "Leaf area density (LAD) describes how leaf area is distributed vertically through the canopy. A LAD profile turns millions of lidar returns into an interpretable structural summary: where vegetation is concentrated, how many canopy layers are present, and how dense those layers are.\n",
    "\n",
    "The workflow calculates LAD profiles for the inventory-linked spatial units and stratifies them by vegetation size class. Local maxima in a profile provide candidate canopy layers. These layers are used to define FATES cohort heights, because FATES represents plants of similar height as cohorts that occupy comparable light environments.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is an important modeling translation. LAD is an observation-derived description of canopy structure; a FATES cohort is a model representation. The workflow uses LAD to construct a biologically informed starting point for FATES.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Expected product:** plot-level LAD profiles and size-class information that describe the initial vertical organization of vegetation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "eae8f8d1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Compute leaf area density (LAD) profiles per plot, stratified by PFT size class\n",
    "prep_lad_path = step(\n",
