    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 3. Learn a plant functional type classifier\n",
    "\n",
    "Can the combination of spectral and structural remote-sensing signals predict the PFT labels observed in the field?\n",
    "\n",
    "A PFT is a model-relevant category that groups plants according to traits or behaviors important to ecosystem processes. The exact PFT scheme depends on the FATES configuration and the research question. It is therefore useful to think of the classifier as configurable infrastructure rather than a species-identification system: the labels, training sites, spatial resolution, and feature representation can all affect the result.\n",
    "\n",
    "This stage builds a supervised learning problem. Inventory and crown information provide the labels; the stacked AOP raster provides the predictor variables; and validation data provide evidence about how well those labels can be recovered from remote sensing."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7b3e37ef-63a2-4f5d-969a-99be29eaea56",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<img src=\"docs/3_workflow.png\" width=\"90%\">"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d8672724",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3a. Prepare the hyperspectral and structural raster stack\n",
    "\n",
    "**Function:** `prep_aop_imagery`\n",
    "\n",
    "Hyperspectral imagery records reflectance in many narrow wavelength bands. Those bands contain information about leaf chemistry, pigments, water content, and canopy composition that can help distinguish functional types. lidar-derived rasters contribute complementary information about height and structure.\n",
    "\n",
    "When flightline hyperspectral data are used, the workflow applies bidirectional reflectance distribution function (BRDF) and topographic corrections to reduce differences caused by viewing geometry, illumination, and terrain. It then stacks the corrected hyperspectral bands with lidar-derived layers so that each pixel has one combined feature vector.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Expected product:** a georeferenced multiband raster in which each pixel has spectral and, where available, structural predictors for classification."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "9adc9ed8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# These remote-sensing steps run for every ic_type except \"field_inv_plots\".\n",
    "if ic_type != \"field_inv_plots\":\n",
    "    # BRDF + topographic correction is only needed for flightline hyperspectral\n",
    "    if hs_type == \"flightline\":\n",
    "        step(correct_flightlines, site=site, year_inv=year, year_aop=year_aop,\n",
    "             data_raw_aop_path=data_raw_aop_path, data_int_path=data_int_path)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Stack hyperspectral bands + lidar-derived rasters into one multi-layer image\n",
    "    stacked_aop_path = step(\n",
    "        prep_aop_imagery, site=site, year=year, hs_type=hs_type,\n",
    "        hs_path=hs_path, tif_path=tif_path, data_int_path=data_int_path,\n",
    "        use_tiles_w_veg=use_tiles_w_veg)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "139a81d7-2d7f-4c22-bf6e-1571c974ba2f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<img src=\"docs/3a_single_multi_raster.png\" width=\"80%\">\n",
    "\n",
    "Visualization of the raster stack used as training data. \n",
    "\n",
    "From \n",
    "https://www.neonscience.org/resources/learning-hub/tutorials/dc-multiband-rasters-r"
   ]
  },
  {
