   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4. Generate FATES initial conditions\n",
    "\n",
    "How can we use pixel-level remote-sensing predictions as the patch and cohort structure inputs expected by FATES?\n",
    "\n",
    "The trained classifier is applied wall-to-wall across the remote-sensing tile. PRISMATIC combines the resulting PFT map with biomass estimates, canopy size classes, and spatial aggregation rules. The result is no longer just a remote-sensing classification: it is a model-ready description of vegetation organized according to FATES's patch and cohort concepts.\n",
    "\n",
    "The final products are two space-delimited files:\n",
    "\n",
    "- The **cohort file** describes the cohorts within each patch, including their PFT identity, height or size class, and associated vegetation quantities.\n",
    "- The **patch file** describes the patches that make up the modeled site and connects them to the cohort records.\n",
    "\n",
    "Parameters such as the number of plots, patch length, minimum distance, spatial resolution, and initial-condition type determine how observations are sampled and aggregated. Treat these as scientific choices as well as technical settings: changing them changes the spatial scale and interpretation of the FATES initialization.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Expected products:** FATES-compatible cohort and patch files, along with wall-to-wall summaries such as above-ground biomass, basal area, leaf area, and stem density.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Where these products are generated:** the code cell below creates the final output directory and passes it to `generate_initial_conditions` as `ic_type_path`:\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "${TUTORIAL_HOME}/data/final/TEAK/2021/rs_wall2wall/\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The `rs_wall2wall` portion comes from `ic_type` in `conf/others/others.yaml`. If you change `ic_type`, the same products are written under the corresponding directory, such as `field_inv_plots`, `rs_inv_plots`, `rs_random_plots`, or `rs_tower_ftpt`.\n",
    "\n",
    "Inside that directory, the R function `generate_pcss_rs` in `initialize/initial_conditions_helper.R` writes:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `ic.css`: the FATES cohort file for the single-site case.\n",
    "- `ic.pss`: the FATES patch file for the single-site case.\n",
    "- `plot_structure_summ.csv`: per-patch summary values for stem density, above-ground biomass, and LAI.\n",
    "- `rs_wall2wall_lai.png`: distribution of patch LAI.\n",
    "- `rs_wall2wall_stemdens.png`: distribution of patch stem density.\n",
    "- `rs_wall2wall_leafbiom.png`: distribution of patch leaf biomass.\n",
    "- `rs_wall2wall_agb.png`: distribution of patch above-ground biomass.\n",
    "- `rs_wall2wall_ba.png`: distribution of patch basal area.\n",
    "\n",
    "These PNGs are diagnostic summaries of the generated patch and cohort structure, rather than wall-to-wall raster maps. The detailed spatial inputs used to create them, including classified PFT rasters, plot geometries, clipped lidar, and LAD files, remain in the `rs_wall2wall/` output directory and its subdirectories. The Python cell prints the returned cohort and patch paths so they can be passed directly to a FATES workflow."
   ]
