    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 5. From initial conditions to FATES simulations\n",
    "\n",
    "The patch and cohort files generated above are inputs, not the endpoint of the ecological analysis. FATES uses them to initialize a dynamic simulation in which cohorts grow, compete for light, recruit, die, and respond to disturbance. Because the initialization contains spatially informed PFT composition and vertical structure, the simulation can begin from a community state grounded in observations rather than from a generic prescribed forest.\n",
    "\n",
    "Comparing simulations initialized from field plots, remote sensing, or different PFT schemes is a useful experiment. Differences in simulated trajectories may reflect the initial vegetation description, the model parameterization, or both. The initial-condition files should therefore be treated as hypotheses about the state of the ecosystem, with their assumptions and uncertainties documented alongside the simulation results.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Questions for discussion\n",
    "\n",
    "- How might a model initialized from a small number of field plots differ from one initialized wall-to-wall with remote sensing?\n",
    "- Which PFTs are well represented in the training data, and which may be underrepresented?\n",
    "- What would you change to apply this workflow to another NEON site or a different disturbance history?\n",
    "- Which validation data would provide the strongest test of the final FATES initialization?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e9b5b024",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Summary and next steps\n",
    "\n",
    "You have followed an observation-to-model workflow comprised of the steps below:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Match and clean NEON field inventory and airborne observations.\n",
    "2. Derive canopy structure and biomass from field data and lidar.\n",
    "3. Train and evaluate a PFT classifier using hyperspectral and structural features.\n",
    "4. Apply the classifier across the tile and aggregate predictions into FATES patches and cohorts.\n",
    "5. Use those files as the starting state for dynamic ecosystem simulations.\n",
    "\n",
    "The most important transferable idea is the structure of the pipeline: **calibration data define the ecological classes, remote sensing provides spatial coverage, validation measures the strength of the connection, and model-specific aggregation turns observations into simulation inputs**.\n",
    "\n",
    "For a next cohort or a new research application, useful extensions include changing the NEON site or year, selecting a different PFT subset, training on multiple sites, testing alternative feature representations, evaluating spatially independent validation data, or comparing remote-sensing-derived initial conditions with field-only initializations."
   ]
